This success has encouraged us to construct a series of isoreticular M’MOFs in which the micropores can be tuned and functionalized by a variety of the following strategies:
a) Incorporation of different secondary organic linkers such as trans-1,4-cyclohexanedicarboxylic acid, $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -biphenyldicarboxylic acid, and 2,6-napthalenedicarboxylic acid.   
b) Immobilization of metal centers other than copper(II) within the metal Schiff base metalloligands.   
c) Incorporation of chiral pockets/environments by using different chiral diamines.   
d) Use of different derivatives of the pyridyl donor moiety such as tert-butyl, phenyl, and cyclohexyl instead of the methyl group.   
e) Incorporation of metal ions other than zinc(II) as the nodes for M’MOFs.
A combination of strategies a and c led to the formation of two isostructural M’MOFs in which the micropores are rationally tuned and functionalized to show enantioselectivity towards small alcohols and highly selective separation of acetylene over ethylene.[11]
As shown in Scheme 3, chiral $^{(R,R)}$ -1,2-cyclohexanediamine was used to construct the preorganized chiral metalloligand $[\mathrm{Cu(SalPyCy)}]$ . The metalloligand $[\mathrm{Cu(SalPyCy)}]$ was treated with either $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bdc}$ or ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{cdc}}$ $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{cdc}=$ trans-1,4,-cyclohexanedicarboxylic acid) along with $Z\mathrm{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ under solvothermal conditions to construct $[Z\mathrm{n}_{3}(\mathrm{bdc})_{3}\{\mathrm{Cu(SalPyCy)\}(\mathrm{G})_{x}]$ (2) and $[Z\mathrm{n}_{3}(\mathrm{cdc})_{3}\{\mathrm{Cu(SalPyCy)\}(\mathbf{G})_{x}]$ (3) with isostructural 3D frameworks. Amazingly, the subtle tuning of the micropores by the change of bdc in 2 to cdc in 3 led to significantly enhanced $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ separation at both 195 and $295\mathrm{K}$ for the activated M’MOF $[Z\mathrm{n}_{3}(\mathrm{cdc)}_{3}\{\mathrm{Cu(SalPyCy)\}]$ (3 a) (Figure 2).[11]
$[\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{Pyac})_{2}]$ is a versatile metalloligand to synthesize different M’MOFs (Scheme 2 b).[8,9] Sakamoto et al. reported a M’MOF $\mathrm{[\{Cu_{2}(p z d c)_{2}C u(P y a c)_{2}H_{2}O^{b}\}{\cdot}4H_{2}O^{c}]_{n}}$ $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}^{\mathrm{b}}=\$ bound water, ${\bf H}_{2}{\bf O}^{\mathrm{c}}={}$ water of crystallization, $\mathrm{\DeltaNa_{2}p z d c=}$ disodium 2,3,-pyrazinedicarboxylate) having active metal sites generated through the complementary coordinationbond rearrangement. The resulting framework retained its permanent porosity, as confirmed by ${\bf N}_{2}$ , $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{:}}$ MeOH, and EtOH sorptions (Figure 3).[8b]
Bloch et al. demonstrated insertion of metal ions into a microporous MOF material lined with $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine moieties to construct microporous M’MOFs (Figure 4 a).[12] Both palladium $(\mathrm{II})$ and copper(II) sites can be immobilized in the pores of the resulting M’MOFs. The microporous M’MOF $\mathrm{[Al(OH)(bpydc)][Cu(BF_{4})_{2}]_{0.97}}$ exhibits much higher $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity (12) compared with the original MOF [Al(OH)- (bpydc)] (2.8), because of the immobilization of copper sites on the pore surfaces and the reduced pores (Figure 4b).
3. Functional M’MOFs for Size, Shape, and Enantioselective Separation of Small Organic Molecules
Kosal et al. reported a M’MOF (PIZA-1) assembled from metalloporphyrins in which $\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{III}}$ porphyrin cores were connected by bridging trinuclear $\mathrm{Co^{II}}$ carboxylate clusters (Figure 5 a).[6] The robustness and integrity of the activated material was confirmed by PXRD and ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption measurements. Size and shape selectivity for different guest molecules were studied by thermal desorption, in which the adsorbed guest molecules were desorbed at elevated temperatures to examine the uptake amount and thus to compare the selectivity. For example, PIZA-1 takes up less aromatic amine when the size of the guest aromatic amine increases; while the selectivity for linear alkyl amines $(\mathbf{C}_{n}\mathbf{H}_{2n+1}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2})$ is dependent on the chain length, in which short-chain amines $(n=4–6)$ were preferably adsorbed over their long-chain $'_{n}=7–10\',$ ) counterparts (Figure 5 b).
The M’MOFs 2 and 3 have been used for chiral recognition and chiral separation of the small alcohol 1- phenylethanol (PEA).[11] The achiral M’MOF 1 encapsulated both $(R)$ - and (S)-PEA into its channels, as confirmed by single-crystal X-ray analysis. In comparison, 3 exclusively took up (S)-PEA in its cavity, thus showing its potential for chiral recognition (Figure 6) of small alcohols. The HPLC analysis showed an uptake of (S)-PEA with an ee value of $21.1\%$ (15.7 and $13.2\%$ for second and third regenerated samples, respectively) for 2, whereas this value increases to
$64\%$ for 3 (55.3 and $50.6\%$ for second and third regenerated samples, respectively). This result was attributed to the smaller pore sizes of 3 compared to 2, which significantly enhanced the enantioselectivity.
A homochiral lamellar solid could be self-assembled from an unsymmetrical Schiff base ligand with one pendent carboxylate moiety and copper(II) nitrate.[13] The ligand used one tridentate ${\bf N}_{2}{\bf O}$ donor site to bind one copper(II) center and one monodentate carboxylate group to bind another copper(II) center to form an H-bonded chiral helical layered structure. This $\mathbf{M}^{\prime}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{F}$ showed enantioselective recognition and separation of racemic secondary alcohols with excellent enantiomeric excess greater than $99.5\%$ .
## 4. Functional M’MOFs as Heterogeneous Asymmetric Catalysts
From the very beginning of the MOF field, it was a dream to use a MOF for selective heterogeneous catalysis. Asymmetric hydrogenation is considered as one of the most efficient strategies to synthesize optically active molecules. The ruthenium and rhodium complexes of binap were found to be very effective for the reduction of a wide range of substrates with high enantioselectivity, but practical industrial applications were often hindered by the high costs and the difficulty in removing leached toxic metals from the organic products.[33] To address these problems, Hu et al. successfully synthesized chiral porous zirconium phosphonates containing Ru binap moieties for enantioselective heterogeneous asymmetric hydrogenation of $\upbeta$ -keto esters (Scheme 4).[7a] The enatiopure metalloligands $\mathrm{[Ru(H_{4}L^{3})(d m f)_{2}C l_{2}]}$ $(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}^{3}=2\mathrm{,}2^{\circ}$ - bis(diphenylphosphanyl)-1,1-binaphthyl-6, $\mathbf{\Omega}_{6}\mathbf{\Omega}_{6}$ -bis(phosphonic acid)) and $[\mathrm{Ru(H_{4}L^{2})(d m f)_{2}C l_{2}}]$ were self-assembled with zirconium salt to form two chiral porous M’MOFs 4 and 5. $\mathbf{M}^{\prime}\mathbf{MOF}\mathbf{4}$ exhibited a total BET surface area[34] of $475\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ with pore volume of $1.02\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , while these values were $387{\mathrm{m}}^{2}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ and $0.53\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for 5. M’MOF 4 was found to catalyze hydrogenation of a wide range of $\upbeta$ -alkyl-substituted $\upbeta$ -keto esters with complete conversions. The ee values ranged from 91.7 to $95.0\%$ , comparable to the homogeneous Ru binap catalyst. This system showed an excellent recyclability for as many as five cycles with complete conversions and high ee values.
A slight modification of the ruthenium-containing metalloligands by incorporation of chelating ligand such as dpen and subsequent complexation with zirconium salt resulted in another two chiral porous M’MOFs, which showed excellent enantioselective heterogeneous asymmetric hydrogenation of aromatic ketones.[7b] $\mathrm{{A0.1}\ m o l\%}$ loading of $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -disubstituted binap M’MOF was found to catalyze a series of aromatic ketones to their corresponding alcohols with remarkably high ee values of $90.6\mathrm{-}99.2\%$ and complete conversions, which were significantly higher than those observed for the parent Ru binap dpen homogeneous catalyst.
A chiral microporous M’MOF $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bpdc})_{2}\mathrm{L}^{4}]$ · $10\mathrm{DMF{\cdot}8H_{2}O}$ 1 $\mathbf{L}^{4}=\left(R,R\right)$ -(2)-1,2-cyclohexanediamino$N,N^{\prime}$ -bis(3-tert-butyl-5-(4-pyridyl)salicylidene) $\mathsf{M n}^{\mathrm{III}}\mathrm{Cl}\ ' $ ) reported by Cho et al. was used as an enatioselective catalyst for olefin epoxidation.[14] A solvothermal reaction between a manganese salen-type metalloligand unit with $Z\mathrm{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ - biphenyldicarboxylic acid led to the formation of this novel M’MOF of doubly interpenetrated $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{a}$ -polonium net (Figure 7).
This M’MOF was thermally stable up to $360^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , and the activated sample retained its crystallinity, thus making it useful to examine its catalytic activity towards asymmetric epoxidation of 2,2-dimethyl- $2H$ -chromene in presence of an oxidant. This M’MOF showed only minor selectivity degradation with $82\%$ ee ( $88\%$ ee for the free metalloligand $\mathrm{L}^{4}$ ) because of the electronic effect arising from binding of pyridyl groups to zinc cations, but had no loss of enantioselectivity and a small loss of activity even after three cycles. The recyclability, easy separation, and extended catalyst lifetime certainly makes this M’MOF a better catalyst than the free metalloligand L4.[14]
Recently, Song et al. reported a family of isoreticular chiral M’MOFs of primitive cubic network topology for asymmetric alkene epoxidation.[15] Five M’MOFs with tunable open channels were synthesized by direct incorporation of three different chiral manganese salen subunits of varied lengths, exhibiting non-interpenetrating frameworks for 7 and 9 and two- or threefold interpenetrating frameworks for 6, 8, and 10 (Figure 8). These M’MOFs were shown to be highly effective in catalyzing enantioselective epoxidation of a variety of unfunctionalized alkenes with up to $92\%$ ee. The rate of conversion was in the increasing order of $\mathbf{6}>\mathbf{10}>\mathbf{8}>$ $\mathbf{7}>\mathbf{9}$ , which is in consistent with the increasing order of the channel sizes. It has been shown that the reaction rate for the interpenetrated M’MOFs depends upon the diffusion rate of alkenes, oxidants, and the final epoxide products into the small open channels. For the case of non-interpenetrated M’MOFs (i.e. with larger open channels), the rate-determining step is limited by intrinsic activity of the catalytic molecular building blocks.
A series of chiral M’MOFs constructed by postsynthetic modification were also prepared for the highly enantioselective asymmetric addition of diethylzinc and/or alkynyl zinc to aldehydes.[16] In these examples of chiral M’MOFs, the chiral organic linkers $(R)\mathbf{-}6\mathbf{,}6^{\prime}$ -dichloro-2,2’-dihydroxy-1,1-binaphthyl-4, $.4^{\prime}$ -bipyridine or binol-derived tetracarboxylic acid were employed to construct homochiral MOFs, while the Lewis acidic $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ catalytic sites were immobilized on the chiral pore surfaces by the postsynthetic reaction of $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O}i\mathrm{Pr})_{4}$ with the phenol functional groups.
## 5. Functional M’MOFs as Chemical Sensors
Xie et al. successfully incorporated highly phosphorescent metalloligands $\mathrm{H_{3}L}^{5}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{L}^{6}$ into M’MOFs $[Z\mathrm{n}_{4}(\upmu^{4}-$ $\mathrm{O)(L^{5})_{2}]\cdot6D M F{\cdot}H_{2}O}$ (11), $\mathrm{\small{[Zn_{3}(L^{6})_{2}(d e f)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{2}]{\cdot D E F{\cdot H_{2}O}}}}$ (12) and $\mathrm{\small{[Zn_{3}(L^{6})_{2}(d m f)(H_{2}O)_{3}]cdot2D M F{\cdot}3H_{2}O}}$ (13) for chemical sensors (Scheme 5).[17] While the permanent porosity of 11 was confirmed by ${\bf N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption measurements, 12 and 13 were nonporous. The most significant observation was molecular $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ sensing with quenching efficiencies of 59, 41, 32, 16, and $8\%$ for 11, 12, 13, $\mathrm{H_{3}L}^{5}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{L}^{6}$ respectively. The Stern–Volmer plot revealed that intensity of 11 dropped to a stable value after each $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ dose (Figure 9 a), and the luminescence was reversibly quenched by molecular $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ (Figure 9 b)
after varying cycles of $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ exposure and removal. In contrast, little irreversible quenching was observed for the two nonporous materials. Thus, efficient and reversible luminescence quenching for the microporous M’MOF material suggested the necessity of fast diffusion of $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ through the open pores.
## 6. Functional M’MOFs as Photoactive Materials
Chandler et al. used luminescent metalloligands $\mathrm{[Ln(L^{7})_{4}]^{5-}}$ ( $\mathrm{\Delta}^{\mathrm{7}}=4{,}4^{\prime}$ -disulfo- $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot N_{}^{\prime}$ -dioxide) to construct 3D M’MOFs with open channels: $[\{\mathsf{N a}_{6}-$ $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}\}\{\mathrm{Ln}(\mathrm{L}^{7})_{4}\}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{n}\mathrm{Cl}]$ and $\mathrm{\small{[Ba_{2}(H_{2}O)_{4}\{L n(L^{7})_{3}(H_{2}O)_{2}\}}.}$ - $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{n}\mathrm{Cl}]$ where $\mathrm{Ln}=\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ , ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Gd}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Dy}^{3+}$ (Scheme 6).[18] Although these structures contracted upon solvent removal, some of the new solids were microporous, as shown by $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , ${\bf N}_{2}$ and water vapor sorption isotherms. The Ln ions with the exception of $\operatorname{Gd}^{3+}$ could be sensitized by the receiver ligand. It was found that luminescence lifetimes depend on hydration and other guests in the channels of the solids.
Kent et al. reported M’MOFs to study $\mathbf{\hat{R}}\mathbf{u}{\rightarrow}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{s}$ energytransfer process that have potential application in lightharvesting materials.[20] The M’MOFs with 0.3, 0.6, 1.4, and $2.6\mathrm{mol}\%$ Os doping were also synthesized to study the energy-transfer dynamics with two-photon excitation at $850\mathrm{nm}$ . It was found that the $\mathtt{R u}$ lifetime at $620\mathrm{nm}$ decreased from 171 ns in the pure $\scriptstyle\mathrm{{Ru}}\mathrm{{MOF}}$ to $29\mathrm{ns}$ in the sample with $2.6\mathrm{mol}\%$ Os doping, with an initial growth in Os emission corresponding to the rate of decay of the Ru excited state (Figure 11).
## 7. Nanoscale M’MOFs for Drug Delivery and Biomedical Imaging
Micrometer- and nanometer-sized particles are very important because of their potential applications in several fields, including catalysis, optics, biosensing, and data storage.[28–31] Rieter et al. reported $\mathrm{\mathbf{P}t}$ -containing nanoscale M’MOF $\mathrm{[Tb}_{2}(\mathrm{dscp})_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{12}]$ (16; dscp $\c=$ disuccinatocisplatin) with a diameter of $(58.3\pm11.3)\mathrm{nm}$ for potential application in anticancer drug delivery.[29] The silica-coated particles 16’ exhibited longer half-lives for dscp release from the particles $(t_{1/2}\approx5.5\mathrm{h}$ for 16’ a with $2\mathrm{nm}$ silica coating, $t_{1/2}{\approx}9\mathrm{h}$ for $\mathbf{16^{\prime}b}$ with $7\mathrm{nm}$ silica coating, and $t_{1/2}\approx1$ h for assynthesized 16). In vitro cancer cell cytotoxicity assays with HT-29 human cells showed that internalized 16’ particles readily released the dscp moieties, thus establishing the anticancer efficiency of these silica-coated particles.
Liu et al. reported phosphorescent nanoscale M’MOFs 17 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}}^{2+})$ and 18 $(Z\mathbf{r}^{4+})$ constructed from metalloligand [Ru$\{5,5^{\prime}{\mathrm{-}}(\mathrm{OOC})_{2}{\mathrm{bpy}}\}({\mathrm{bpy}})_{2}]$ $(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{bpy}}=2,2^{\prime}$ -bipyridine) and $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}/\$ ${\cal Z}\mathbf{r}^{4+}$ salts, which exhibited high dye loadings of 78.7 and $57.4\%$ , respectively.[30] Compound 18 was stable in water but rapidly decomposed in phosphate-buffered saline (PBS) at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with a half-life of about $0.5\mathrm{h}$ . To slow down the release of dye molecules in biologically relevant media, particles of 18 were coated with a thin shell of silica to get $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@{\bf18}$ particles, which have a longer half life of $3.2\mathrm{h}$ . To prevent particle aggregation, increase the dispersibility of nanoparticles, and target certain biomarkers, $\mathbf{SiO}_{2}@\mathbf{18}$ particles were further coated with $\mathrm{CH_{3}O P E G2000-S i(O E t)_{3}}$ and anisamide$\mathrm{PEG2000–Si(OEt)_{3}}$ to afford PEGylated and targeted particles, $\mathbf{PEG-SiO}_{2}@\mathbf{18}$ and AA-PEG- $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{SiO}_{2}@\mathbf{18}$ ( $\mathbf{PEG}=$ poly(ethylene glycol), $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{A}=$ anisamide), respectively. The nanoscale coordination polymers (NCPs) were tested for their potential usage as contrast agents for optical imaging. H460 lung cancer cells were incubated with PEG- $\mathbf{SiO}_{2}@\mathbf{18}$ and AAPEG- $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathbf{18}$ particles for $24\mathrm{h}$ , and no appreciable cell death was observed (Figure 12 d). Confocal fluorescence microscopy studies (Figure $\bar{12}\mathrm{a-c}\rangle$ revealed significant MLCT luminescent signal when H460 cells were incubated with AA-PEG- $\mathbf{SiO}_{2}@\mathbf{18}$ particles. In contrast, less luminescence intensity was observed for the nontargeted PEG$\mathbf{SiO}_{2}@\mathbf{18}$ particles. Furthermore, enhanced dye uptake was observed for AA-PEG- $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{SiO}_{2}@\mathbf{18}$ (Figure 12 e) particles in comparison with either 18 or PEG- $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{SiO}_{2}@\mathbf{18}$ particles.
## 8. Conclusion and Outlook
Construction of functional mixed metal–organic frameworks (M’MOFs) is still in an early stage. The bright promise of this new metalloligand approach to assemble M’MOF materials with functional sites for the recognition of small molecules will initiate extensive research on the exploration and discovery of new functional M’MOF materials. It is foreseen that a variety of novel M’MOFs will be realized for applications in gas storage and separation, enantioselective separation, heterogeneous catalysis, sensing, and as photoactive and nanoscale drug delivery and biomedical imaging materials in the near future.
